    "body": "I've done some digging and I've got an idea on how to proceed with this, but determining if a directory is writable in a straightforward and cross platform way is messier than I thought. Because of that I was hoping to get some feedback on my idea?\r\n\r\nIn Windows, determining if a directory is write-protected is rather straightforward, since to my knowledge a write-protected directory is simply one with the \"read-only\" box checked in its properties. Fortunately, Rust exposes a method called [readonly](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/fs/struct.Permissions.html#method.readonly) specifically for this information.\r\n\r\nIn macOS/Linux systems, the situation is a bit more complicated, since write-protected could mean the directory simply has no write permissions at all (`r--r--r--`), _or_ that the current user doesn't have sufficient permissions. Unfortunately, it does not seem that Rust provides any mechanism to immediately determine if the user has sufficient permissions.\r\n\r\nSpaceship-prompt, being written in zsh, can check directly if the directory is writable by using [this](https://github.com/denysdovhan/spaceship-prompt/blob/50e371f5b7b14922c4c2492ef9c7be1095064cb7/sections/dir.zsh#L59) comparison. I think the easiest way to port this to Rust would be to spawn a `sh` process and run the same comparison directly. Here is a [gist showing a minimal example](https://gist.github.com/JDSeiler/8fc10f36adaac8fc5607e25c82995724) of running such a command and checking its status code. \r\n\r\nThis method has problems, namely making it cross platform would require writing multiple versions of this check and running the correct one based on what shell the user is running, not to mention future maintenance. However, as far as I know, the primary alternative would be to pull in libc as a dependency and use a combination of [PermissionsExt](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/os/unix/fs/trait.PermissionsExt.html#tymethod.mode), `stat`, and `ps` (< which would require spawning subprocesses anyway), to determine if the current user has sufficient write permissions. In comparison to the `libc` method, I think executing shell code from Rust has a few benefits: \r\n\r\n1. Easier to understand logic: Each shell comparison itself would be a simple if statement, and matching on exit code is also straightforward. In comparison the `libc` method requires cryptically named bitmasks and 2 other programs (stat and ps) to get all the required information.\r\n2. No extra dependency: Spawning sub-processes is built into Rust.\r\n3. No mystery-meat C code from libc: All code would be Rust code or very simple, no side-effect shell code.\r\n\r\nSo far this is the best plan I've come up with, but I'm very interested to hear what more experienced individuals think or what other solutions people might have.\r\n",
    "author_association": "NONE",
